<!-- Airhub vs ByteSIM Comparison Content -->
<div class="content-section">

  {% set airhubBest = providerA.codes | bestValidCode %}
  {% set bytesimPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Airhub</strong> if you want SMS and a local number handed to you automatically with a cheap data plan, no extra steps. Pick <strong>ByteSIM</strong> if you specifically need a real number that can make and receive calls, and you don't mind uploading a passport photo and a selfie to get it. Airhub's number is a bundled extra; ByteSIM's is the actual product.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: A Bundled Extra vs a Verified Real Number</h2>
  <p>
    Airhub folds a local phone number into most of its data plans without asking for any identity verification, but that number only receives SMS. It doesn't make or take calls, so it covers things like a verification text or giving out a number that isn't your personal one, not a phone-number replacement. ByteSIM goes further in select countries (the US, Canada, UK, France, Spain, Australia, Vietnam, South Korea, and Thailand): its number plans bundle in calls, texts, hotspot sharing, and a local IP address, a genuinely different product from a data-only eSIM. The catch is that ByteSIM's own site is explicit that local-number plans require identity verification, a passport photo and a selfie, per local telecom regulation, while its plain data-only eSIMs don't. Airhub's bundled number, by contrast, doesn't ask for any ID at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    That trade-off shows up in the codes, too. Airhub's own affiliate code isn't currently applying a discount at checkout (we've reported it and expect it fixed), so ESIMOFFER5 is the Airhub code we actually recommend right now. ByteSIM's TPDBYTE is advertised elsewhere as 10% off, but tested on a real order it only saved about 5%, roughly matching the automatic Summer Sale ByteSIM already applies without any code. Neither provider's headline discount is quite what it first appears to be.
  </p>

