      <li><strong>ByteSIM:</strong> {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, advertised as 10% off but tested at roughly 5% in practice. <a href="{{ '/' + providerBKey + '/' }}">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Neither discount is large. If the code amount is the deciding factor for you, it shouldn't be, since both providers' real value is in what the plan itself includes rather than a percentage off.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Airhub or ByteSIM the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Airhub if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You just want an SMS-capable number bundled in without handing over ID</li>
        <li>You're headed somewhere Airhub prices sharply, like Thailand or Japan</li>
        <li>You don't need to actually make or receive phone calls on the number</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with ByteSIM if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You need a real number that can call and text, not just receive SMS</li>
        <li>Your destination is one of ByteSIM's supported local-number countries</li>
        <li>You're fine completing ID verification for that specific plan</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Neither of these is the only option if a real phone number isn't the point at all: <a href="/alosim/">AloSIM</a> bundles a free Hushed virtual number with no ID check required, and <a href="/simlocal/">SimLocal</a>'s UK plans include a real number with calls guaranteed, which we bought and confirmed ourselves. For eSIM device compatibility, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Airhub vs ByteSIM FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does Airhub's local number make calls</h3>
      <p>No. Airhub's bundled number receives SMS but doesn't handle voice calls. ByteSIM's local-number plans, in its supported countries, include actual calls alongside texts and hotspot sharing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Do I need ID verification for either provider's number</h3>
      <p>ByteSIM requires a passport photo and a selfie for its local-number plans specifically, per local telecom regulation; its plain data-only eSIMs don't require this. Airhub's bundled SMS number doesn't ask for ID verification.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Airhub or ByteSIM</h3>
      <p>Airhub, for data-only pricing: it undercuts most competitors in destinations like Thailand and Japan. ByteSIM's plans that include a real, callable phone number cost more, since you're paying for a genuinely different product, not just data.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Is either discount code worth much</h3>
      <p>Not really. Airhub's working code, ESIMOFFER5, is 5% off. ByteSIM's TPDBYTE is advertised as 10% off but tested on a real order only saved about 5%, similar to ByteSIM's own automatic Summer Sale. Pick either provider for what the plan includes, not for the code.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

